From 04d7330c9e1c827fdca62d0e445d55cac2a89183 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alberto L <alberto_leon@seas.harvard.edu>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 22:29:28 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] feat(sender): optional visible alert mode for interactive
 wakes

By default every wake is a silent background push (APNs
content-available, FCM data-only). On iOS that is best effort: Apple
throttles background notifications ("don't try to send more than two or
three per hour") and "If something force quits or kills the app, the
system discards the held notification". A wake a person is waiting on,
such as an approval, cannot depend on that.

With GATEWAY_ALERT_LOC_KEY set, an interactive wake is sent as a
notification the platform displays itself: an APNs alert push (priority
10, apns-collapse-id, aps.alert.loc-key) and an FCM notification message
(android.notification.body_loc_key, collapse_key). It stays contentless:
the payload names a localisation key, never text, and the device shows
its own string for it; the wake's hint fields ride along unchanged.
Background wakes, and every wake with the mode unset, are unchanged.

- sender: VisibleAlert (validated keys; collapse id at most 64 bytes),
  the pure request builders apns_request and fcm_message, and
  with_visible_alert on the APNs and FCM senders
- main: GATEWAY_ALERT_LOC_KEY, GATEWAY_ALERT_TITLE_LOC_KEY,
  GATEWAY_ALERT_COLLAPSE_ID (default "wake"),
  GATEWAY_FCM_ANDROID_CHANNEL_ID; an invalid value stops startup
- README: the mode, with Apple's reasons
- tests: key validation; with the mode unset both payloads are
  unchanged; an interactive wake names only the key and flags; a
  background wake stays silent with the mode on; the title key; the FCM
  notification form

Signed-off-by: Alberto L <alberto_leon@seas.harvard.edu>
